  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Rehabilitation of 2 Hydropow.plants in Mozambique - Monitoring</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Reparation av 2 vattenkraftverk i Mo&#xe7;ambique</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Repair of two hydropower plants in the central part of Mozambique. The main objective of the project is to provide sustainable energy for poverty reduction. The specific objectives are to manage the domestic future electricity demands of Mozambique and to increase the export of surplus electricity during periods of low demand.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Reparation av tv&#xe5; vattenkraftverk i den centrala delen av Mo&#xe7;ambique.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Mavuzi and Chicamba plants were constructed in 1956 and 1969, respectively, with an initial installed capacity of approximately 52MW for Mavuzi and 48MW for Chicamba. However, due to aging infrastructure and damage inflicted during the civil war between 1976 and 1992, both plants suffered from wear and tear and sabotage of equipment.
The intervention main goals are the rehabilitation and upgrade of the Mavuzi and Chicamba Hydro Power Plants and dams to ensure long-term, reliable and renewable electricity supply to the population in central Mozambique hence leading to improved livelihoods, more productivity and poverty reduction.
The specific objectives of this contribution are to manage the domestic future electricity demands of Mozambique and to increase the export of surplus electricity during periods of low demand.
To reach these objectives, the following works were carried out on the two Hydro power plants:
Mavuzi:
Refurbishment of the dam comprising hydraulic steel structure civil and electrical
works;
Refurbishment of pressure tunnel including penstock;
 Refurbishment of power house internal and external part for civil, electrical
including traveling crane;
 Complete Replacement of units 1 and 2 rated 5 MW, new turbines, governors,
pressure release valve, generators, excitation systems
 Refurbishment of unit 3, 4 and 5 rated 15 MW including the supply of new
turbine including the control and excitation system
 Removement and replacement of a new Generator fire extinguishing System
 Replacement of the two auxiliary transformers rated 400KVA, 6.6/0.4KV with new ones
Chicamba:
 Refurbishment of the dam comprising hydraulic steel structure civil and
electrical works
 Refurbishment of the interior and exterior of the power house for civil, electrical
including traveling crane
 Refurbishment of unit 1 and 2 rated 25 MW including the servomotors and
operating mechanism, excitation system
Page 5 of 18
 Replacement of the two existing Power transformers rated 25MVA 6.6/110KV
and one auxiliary transformer 400KVA 6.6/22KV with two new ones rated
25MVA 6.6/22/110KV.
 Refurbishment of the Elevator at Chicamba HPP
 Replacement of the two auxiliary transformer rated 400KVA, 6.6/0.4KV with
two new ones.
At the end of the intervention, it is expected that both Hydro Power Plants (HPPs) will work at full capacity and supply energy to the central region of Mozambique, with sufficient reliability, in the affected areas.
The two HPPs supply electricity to more than 300 thousand customers between households, basic services, business and commercial services in the provinces of Sofala, Manica and Tete and to the neighboring countries of Zimbabwe and Malawi.
The whole central region (provinces of Manica, Sofala, Tete and Zamb&#xe9;zia) is heavily dependent on these HPPs for electricity supply. In addition, the region hosts large areas of agriculture, fisheries, forests, wildlife and businesses already in expansion such as tourism, transport corridors for inland countries, export and import through the ports and airports, all of which rely on electricity to grow and develop.
The tropical cyclone IDAI which affected Mozambique and the Mavuzi area, mid-March 2019, strongly affected the country, the infrastructure, the energy supply and distribution, as well as the completion of the rehabilitation project. The cyclone caused an extreme flood event which resulted in over topping of the left and right abutment of the Mavuzi dam, and of the flood gates, thereby causing damage to some of the installations at the dam. Hence the greater part of 2021 was dedicated to the repair works of the damage caused by the cyclone. At the end of 2021 further problems were identified during a site visit which were oil spillages at the two dam sites and problems with the stator windings of the Mavusi unit 3 generator. This caused the project to enter the third phase for the repairs to take place.</narrative>
  </description>
